### A Brief Look at This Week's Recall Stats

Since the start of the year, 419 recalls have been logged, with 103 of them being Class I recalls--the most serious category, where there's a reasonable probability that the product will cause serious health problems or death.

Two major offenders this week include California Dairies Inc. and Silver Moon LP (dba Loard's Ice Cream). California Dairies Inc. has had Class I recalls for potential Salmonella contamination in their non-fat dried milk powder. And it's not just one batch--we're talking paper bags, plastic totes, you name it. Who knew milk powder could be such a risky business?

Silver Moon LP has been keeping the recall train rolling with undeclared allergens--everything from milk to yellow dye #5 in their various ice cream and sherbet products. Check your freezers, folks.

### The Honey is Not So Sweet

Walker Honey Co has pulled their honey spreads off the shelf due to potential contamination with stainless steel dust, flakes, and even small particles of plastic. Not the kind of crunchy texture you'd expect in your morning tea or toast.

### A Repeat Offender Highlight

Moonlight Packing Corporation, currently holding a score of 96 (that's an F, for clarity), remains in the spotlight with 11 Class I recalls this year. If you're keeping track, that's 100% of their recalls being the most serious kind. Food safety isn't a suggestion, folks.

### New Brands on the Radar

California Dairies Inc. and Raw Farm LLC have been added to the list of 'brands to watch' this week. The former for their Salmonella woes and the latter for their E. coli O157:H7 contamination in raw cheddar products. Pro tip: "raw" isn't a badge of honor, it's a warning label.

### Looking Ahead

The year is not even halfway done, and we're nearing 420 recalls (pun intended, though unfunny given the context). If this pace continues, 2026 might set new records. Is it time for the food industry to clean up its act? Or do we just need to start growing all our own food at home? Either way, stay vigilant.

### What Can You Do?

Check your products against the recall list, know the signs of contamination and spoilage, and don't be shy about returning potentially unsafe products. It's your stomach--protect it.
